START-HERE -- a dozen things to type, and how to get back out==============================================================The disk is alphabetical and long.  This is a short list of programs thatrun with nothing set up: no arguments, no data files to find, no terminaltype to get right.  It exists so that "does this thing work?" has an answeryou can reach in one line.`about <name>' will tell you what any of them is, where it came from, whatit reads and whether its source is here.PRINTS SOMETHING AND STOPS--------------------------  fortune        a quotation  cookie         another one, from a different collection  cal            this month, and the two either side  ascii          the character set, four ways  today          the date in words, with the phase of the moon  banner HELLO   large letters  ttyexp         a firework goes off and leaves a clean screenfortune and cookie read collections posted to usenet in the 1980s.  Theyare as they arrived and nobody has been through them.RUNS UNTIL YOU STOP IT----------------------  rain           rain on the screen  worms          worms across the screen  life           Conway's lifeThese hold the terminal.  Ctrl-C ends all three.TAKES A WHILE-------------  advent         Colossal Cave.  Type WEST to begin.  hack           the dungeon.  `?' for keys, `Q' then `y' to quit.  larn           another dungeon.  Ctrl-C to quit.  bog            Boggle.  Ctrl-C to quit.  backgammon     it explains itself if you ask for instructions  card           a Christmas card carried by a Towers of Hanoi -- the                 twelve disks are the twelve lines of the message.                 4095 moves, so Ctrl-C when you have seen enough.GETTING OUT-----------Ctrl-C ends nearly everything here.  The ones that want something else sayso, above.  If a program has taken the terminal and Ctrl-C does not reachit, Ctrl-E then Ctrl-C is os9exec's own interrupt.THESE WERE TRIED, not taken on trust.  Measured 2026-08-29: hack's `Q'then `y' quits and prints the high-score table, and Ctrl-C ends larn,bog, card, backgammon, rain, worms and life -- which is what theplay-tests exercise every time they run.WHERE TO GO NEXT----------------  DOC/CATEGORIES     everything, grouped by what it is for  DOC/INDEX          everything, alphabetical, one line each  DOC/README-RUNNING three ways to run this disk  about <name>       one program, in fullIf you want to keep one on your own system rather than run it from here,`keep <name>' copies the program and the files it reads.  DOC/README-KEEPexplains what it records and how `drop' undoes it.